WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.400
- So a common phrase
in the video world is,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.200
eh, we'll fix it in post.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.900
Meaning the shot isn't quite perfect,

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.723
but we'll just fix that
stuff in post-production,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.130
kinda like this.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.431
(drums rolling)

00:00:13.431 --> 00:00:15.210
(magical music)

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.530
There you go.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.430
Or how about a little bit of this too?

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:19.618
(drums rolling)

00:00:19.618 --> 00:00:22.200
(magical music)

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.610
There we go, much better.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.010
See, it's not that it's
a lazy way of doing it,

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.300
but I feel like a good filmmaker

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.480
tries to get as reliably good in camera

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.793
as possible instead of
relying on software.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.640
And the we'll fix it in
post version of a smartphone

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.320
is definitely the Google Pixel.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.720
So many of the Pixel's best features

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.210
are literally fixing it in post, right?

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.910
Oh, is your face blurry
in that picture you took?

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.410
Fine, Face Unblur.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.550
Fix it in post.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.383
Oh, what's that?

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:53.490
You didn't quite get that
angle right on your photo?

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:54.480
Magic Editor.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.620
Fix it in post.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.150
Oh, was there a weird background audio

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.350
in that video you took?

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.020
Easy, we got Audio Eraser, fix it in post,

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.080
and many of these strategies
with a little help from AI,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.110
actually work and that's
where the Pixel shines.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.510
But there's also some
things about smartphones

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.960
that you kind of just
can't really fix in post.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:13.860
First things first.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.900
The design on the outside of these phones

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.430
looks, I would say, familiar.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.110
It looks like the past few years of Pixel

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.520
which I actually really like,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.290
but I also do think this
one's a little bit better

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.123
in a few ways.

00:01:23.123 --> 00:01:24.840
First of all, the camera circles

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.020
did the iPhone Dynamic Island thing

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.450
of combining themselves
into one big circle

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.240
which I actually like with
these new bigger sensors here.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.550
And then also the Pro Pixels now have

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.050
a completely flat display,

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.330
so finally moving away from
the slightly curved edges

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.930
from the past years and a
satin back in every color

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.840
which does a shockingly good
job at hiding fingerprints,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.880
if your team know case like I am.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:48.713
Aside from that though,

00:01:48.713 --> 00:01:50.340
everything else is right
where you'd expect it.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.430
The one thing I wanna see
if maybe they can fix now

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.080
is the dust that always accumulates

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.120
like right under and above the camera bar.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.380
Probably the only way to get rid of that

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.330
is to sort of curve it like they did

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.580
on the Oppo Find X4 and X3,

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:03.780
but I don't know if they'll ever do that.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.340
But the biggest improvement
to the hardware though

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.160
is definitely upfront,

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.820
honestly to the point where
I feel like these new screens

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.190
sort of tie the phone together
in a way that they're up

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.620
at the level of any other flagship phone.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.020
So the smaller Pixel 8 now has

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.750
a 6.2-inch 1080P OLED display,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.550
slightly smaller than last year.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.380
And that display is surrounded now

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.810
by perfectly thin even
bezels all the way around,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.270
no more tiny chin like previous years.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.970
And it's now bumped up
to 120 hertz display

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:37.140
that gets up to a new maximum
2,000 nits of brightness

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.130
which is incredible.

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.210
It's super bright.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.490
They're naming it the Actua display

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.350
because they feel the
need to name everything.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.720
But I love how it's visible all the time.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.240
Auto brightness has also been
behaving this time around

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.500
which is great.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:50.640
It's not LTPO though,

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.380
so you'll still probably wanna turn off

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.450
the always on display to
save a bit of battery.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.800
But then the Pixel 8 Pro
has a new 6.7-inch display.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.860
It is LTPO, it's closer to 1440P

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.670
and it maxes out at 2,400
nits max brightness.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.350
This one is legitimately up there

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.660
with the brightest displays I've ever seen

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.130
on a smartphone in person

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:14.130
and I definitely did not expect
that to come from a Pixel.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.680
They're naming this the
Super Actua display.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.020
So the result is a hardware package

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.100
on the outside of the phone

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.290
that honestly feels like you can put it

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.050
right up alongside any
flagships from Xiaomi,

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.390
Samsung, or anyone else, you name it.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.730
Just from the way it's
built, the way it feels,

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.770
the metal rails, the satin back,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.680
the clickiness of the buttons,
the super bright display,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.303
the even bezels, everything about it,

00:03:36.303 --> 00:03:39.660
it's just this is a rare
W for Pixel hardware.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.790
And then two other small things
about this display, though.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.340
One, it's still just a single
front-facing camera at the top

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.590
but Google has found a way with software

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.930
to get more information out of this camera

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.210
in order to be able to
use it for secure unlock

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.760
for things like banking apps
and logging into things.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.790
So basically instead of
adding more hardware up front

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:01.380
like an infrared camera or
a dot projector or radar,

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.440
if you remember that,
they just fix it in post

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.180
with software magic and it's convenient.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.440
But then number two,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.230
the fingerprint reader has not improved.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.270
So remember we were talking
about this in the past few years

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.850
of Pixels, all the best,
the highest end flagships

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:15.683
feels like they all have

00:04:15.683 --> 00:04:17.790
that super fast ultrasonic
fingerprint reader.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:22.140
This one is still optical
and still just a little bit,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.270
just a beat slower than the high end ones.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:27.360
It's not horrible, but you
can tell it's not as fast

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.390
or as consistently accurate
with like rain on your phone

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:31.800
as something like a Samsung phone.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.450
It's also not any bigger, either.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:34.770
But hey, as long as we're
talking about the Pixel,

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:35.610
we might as well just get

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:36.780
right into talking about the camera

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.760
because this is where you're gonna see

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:43.760
the fix it in post pushed
to its absolute maximum,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.060
more than any other phone.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.220
So the new camera
sensors across the board,

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.960
they do plenty of heavy
lifting, of course,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.660
it's a big 50-megapixel primary sensor

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.060
shared across both the 8 and the 8 Pro,

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.770
F1.7 OIS and Pixel photos, they look good,

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.770
they're sharp, there's tons
of dynamic range as expected.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.330
Auto focus is really fast

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.010
and it can get a pretty
shallow depth of field

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.900
with close up subjects, which is nice.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:09.030
But really most of what you're looking at

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:12.570
when you look at a Pixel
photo or video is software.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.610
So the multiple exposures blended together

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.470
into a single HDR shot,

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:19.740
the strategic relighting
of recognized faces.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.060
I also, I do feel like there's

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.830
a little extra noise reduction this year.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.740
So sometimes details
get kind of watercolory

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.570
but at least there's enough resolution

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:29.880
that it doesn't look
terrible when zoomed out.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.680
And I've also found there
is definitely a tendency

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.470
to over-HDR some images sometimes.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.260
And when it comes to
features, well, oh my god,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:38.580
there are so many now.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.190
You probably already
know about Magic Eraser.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:42.023
Look, oh, what's that?

00:05:42.023 --> 00:05:43.950
You've taken a photo and
someone's in the background,

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:45.120
you wanna get rid of 'em.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.970
Boom, Magic Eraser, fix it in post.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:48.803
Oh, what's that?

00:05:48.803 --> 00:05:51.150
You have a photo where someone's
moving a little too fast

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.640
or the hands were shaky and
the face is kind of blurry.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.310
Well, there's a feature
called Face Unblur,

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:57.570
literally fix it in post.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:00.090
And then even this new
one called The Best Take,

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.920
people with kids are gonna love this one.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.470
Basically, you know how
anytime you take a group photo

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.090
where there's like five or
six people in the photo,

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.700
you just blast, you just
take a bunch of pictures

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.370
and inevitably, one person's face,

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.530
they're either blinking
or sneezing or something.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:14.363
It's never perfect.

00:06:14.363 --> 00:06:17.160
But this feature will let
you go into that group shot.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.320
And this is for any group
shot you've ever taken

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.880
in your Google Photos library, by the way,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.560
not just a shot on the Pixel.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.530
And it automatically
recognizes the burst of photos

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:28.560
and lets you pick the best
face from the whole burst

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:31.050
on each person in the photo

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.360
with surprisingly impressive accuracy.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.520
You will genuinely never
have that one person

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.170
with their eyes closed
or looking the wrong way

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:38.880
in a group photo ever again.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.490
I guess that makes your
holiday cards that much easier.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:42.840
But also even in videos, you know,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.690
I've taken a lot of videos
on the Pixel 8 and 8 Pro,

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:47.610
there's a lot of processing happening.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:50.700
I do think Pixel video is
improved over last year.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:52.350
It's less noisy in the shadows

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.120
and there's some nice
shallow depth of field

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.070
with quick autofocus.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:07:00.150
I shot almost the entire
Corvette Z06 autofocus video

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.550
on the Pixel 8 Pro and I
think it looks really good,

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:05.760
but also in a lot of the shots of my face,

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.820
there's this artificial
brightening processing,

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.940
like you can see it in my
footage before I start talking.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:15.330
When it lands on my face, how
it just brightens up my face

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.390
and adds this contrast and
just throughout the footage,

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:22.200
there is a good amount of
processed sharpening going on.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:24.870
Also, the Audio Eraser video feature

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.780
is all right, I would say.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.330
Again, you can only fix so much in post,

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:32.040
but basically they show this feature

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:33.240
where if you're taking videos

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.340
and there's some background noise,

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:36.750
you can go into the editor

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:40.140
and separate out like the
layers of different sounds.

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:41.880
So there's this really cool UI

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.340
that they've built in
that is super impressive.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.830
You go into Edit Audio and Audio Eraser.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:50.580
It analyzes the video and
then lets you drag up or down

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.490
levels of different
sounds it's identified.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:57.570
So nature and voices and background sounds

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.630
and wind, you know, depends
on what video you took.

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:04.950
It does okay with small
stuff, but like, I don't know,

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:06.330
it's super impressive at a low level,

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:09.390
but then it can't really
save the most disruptive wind

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.700
around our studio or the exhaust sound

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:14.280
of a five and a half liter
naturally aspirated V8.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:16.020
Like it's not magic.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.220
About this in the main channel

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.350
in the Rimac Nevera video.

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:20.940
That car is electric

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.580
and basically has multiple personalities

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.500
and this one kind of does in the same way.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.480
You've probably heard the other cliche of.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:29.130
I will say shout out to the colors though.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:32.460
Honestly the color in
Pixel video is really good.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.040
If you told me I could only take pictures

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:38.520
by taking stills from
video of any smartphone,

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:40.560
I think I would pick the Pixel 8 Pro.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.510
Just the footage looks so good.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:46.140
Now Google, here's the
thing about being so good

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:50.280
at software visibly is it
becomes way more obvious

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:53.400
when you're suddenly not able
to pull off a software trick

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.160
on the non-Pro phone that
you are able to pull off

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.920
on the Pro phone, even though
they're the same computer.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:03.090
Like the good thing that you
did was put the same exact chip

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:06.330
the Tensor G3 with the
same image signal processor

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:10.230
and the same primary camera
and the same selfie camera

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:12.480
in the pro and the non-Pro phones.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.180
So this sort of artificial separation

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:19.833
between the Pro and the non-Pro
with like software locking,

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.020
it was obvious when Apple did it

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.450
and it's even more obvious
when Google does it.

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:25.440
So if you open up the camera

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.180
and switch over to Pro Controls,

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:29.760
that's only available on the Pro phone.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:30.593
Now, that's funny.

00:09:30.593 --> 00:09:32.430
Of course, you can argue the non-Pro phone

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:36.030
doesn't need Pro controls, but
it's clearly capable of them.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:38.250
Even if you bury them in
settings, it's the same computer.

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:41.100
They also both have a 50-megapixel sensor,

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.230
but because it's locked
under the Pro mode,

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:46.860
you can only take 50-megapixel
shots on the Pro phone.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:48.690
The non-Pro phone is always binning.

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:50.070
Theoretically that's
gonna be one of the things

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:52.020
that you'll have to pay
the 300 extra dollars

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.940
to get up to the Pro phone for,

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:57.150
but also someone's already
ported it to the Pixel 6.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.700
So like, come on, Google, you ain't slick.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:01.560
There's even a blog post
about some other features

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:02.820
that are coming to the Pixel.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.010
They have this thing called Zoom Enhance.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:08.700
That's basically a generative
fill version of Super Res Zoom

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.440
that's coming later this year

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:13.140
only to Pixel 8 Pro for some reason.

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.840
And especially improved
MagicERraser version

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:17.880
that uses Generative Fill

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:20.520
on the 8 Pro and only the 8 Pro.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:21.990
- It's ridiculous.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:23.820
It's obvious what's being done out here.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.290
It's a nightly basis.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:26.610
I hope the world can see now

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:27.527
what's really going on out here

00:10:27.527 --> 00:10:29.340
'cause it's getting ridiculous.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:30.510
It's really ridiculous.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:31.950
- Okay, last camera thing.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:35.820
Magic Editor is...

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:36.653
It's wild.

00:10:36.653 --> 00:10:38.910
It's one of the wildest
things they've ever made.

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:40.770
It is available on both phones

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:43.890
and it definitely goes
beyond fixing it in post.

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:46.110
So you open up a photo, you hit Edit,

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:47.640
and then at the bottom left corner,

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:51.540
there's this wonderful colorful
rainbow Magic Editor button

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:53.340
just tempting you to press it.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:56.310
From here, the editor
UI is basically saying,

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:59.640
all right, go ahead and select
something with your hands

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:00.750
and then we can change it.

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:02.040
We can do whatever you want to it.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:05.790
Select an object and you can
remove it, move it around,

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:09.330
resize it, select a person,
we can move 'em up or down

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:13.170
or hit the bottom middle button
to switch the whole scene

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:16.950
to golden hour or replace
the entire sky in your photo.

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:18.690
And then once you submit the edit

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:20.220
it takes a few seconds to process,

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:21.360
and then kind of like DALL·E,

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.910
it gives you four options of this edit

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:26.460
to pick whichever version
is the most convincing.

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.520
They're not, you know, professional
photographer level edits

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.230
meaning you can zoom in and Pixel peep

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:34.260
and find some edges sometimes
that look weird here or there.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:37.860
I also did try the exact
same Generative Fill example

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:39.480
that they showed in their commercials

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:40.800
of this kid on a park bench

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.440
where it oppressively fills
in the missing balloons.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.020
When I do it, it kind of tries to do that,

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:49.020
but definitely doesn't
do it nearly as well.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:52.020
But the truth is zoomed all the way out,

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:55.470
these are decently convincing
edits to regular people

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:57.570
and you can just do it with
any photo in your library,

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:00.060
so it really just feels
like without knowing

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:01.290
how to use Photoshop

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:03.990
or any of these image generation AI tools,

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:06.510
the Pixel Magic Editor,
basically what it did

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:11.510
is just it lowered the barrier
to entry to just lie I guess.

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:14.610
Now every once in a while,
I did get a popup box

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:18.450
over my edit attempt that said
it's unable to show results

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:22.140
because it might violate our gen AI terms.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:24.540
But I was never able to
find like exactly why

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:26.130
or any trend in this.

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:27.690
It seemed to come up more often

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:29.550
when I tried to edit
a picture of a person,

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:31.920
but then it would totally let
me just select a watermark

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.350
in a photo and effortlessly delete that,

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.320
which is probably gonna be
concern for some photographers.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:39.510
So yeah, I would just say
that I'm probably gonna end up

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:42.120
making an entire separate
video just about this tool

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:43.020
'cause it's fascinating

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.620
and what it brings into the world is wild.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:48.420
But I don't know.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.290
What is a photo?

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:52.050
But you know what you
can't fix in post, though?

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:53.130
Battery life.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:58.080
You can't AI your way out
of a huge 2,400 nit screen

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:58.980
burning through battery.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:00.480
You're just gonna need the hardware,

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:02.010
you're gonna need a big battery

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:03.960
you're gonna need an
efficient chip for this one.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:05.730
And I'm happy to report

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.850
that I have had really good
battery life on the Pixels.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:10.380
And this is especially on the 8 Pro.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:13.050
So I've gotten six to eight
hours of screen on time

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:15.690
regularly, standby time has been decent.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:19.140
My only real question at this
point is, and I only ask this

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:21.150
because of the history
we've seen with the Pixel,

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:24.420
how long will the battery
life be this good?

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:27.180
Because, you know, it could
be great for a few months

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:29.340
and then start to fade
like previous Pixels,

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:30.810
or maybe it'll be great for years,

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:32.400
the whole time you own the phone.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:33.300
Only time will tell.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.610
At the end of the day though,
the Pixel is still great

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:38.700
at what it's always been great
at, which is being smart.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:40.380
Like there are so many features,

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:43.770
from call screening, to
Google Assistant everywhere,

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.930
Google's stock apps getting so good.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.150
And the more I use the
Pixel as my daily phone,

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:49.500
as I have for so long,

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.600
the more I just kind of
get used to this stuff

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:54.210
and the harder it is to live
without it on another phone.

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:55.800
Android 14 has already breathed life

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:57.420
into older pixels already.

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.160
Literally my biggest primary bug

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:02.040
is clicking Twitter
notifications, for some reason,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:05.010
Just take an extra beat
and a half to open.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:05.843
Kind of weird.

00:14:05.843 --> 00:14:08.040
One of the new features on this one

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.110
is when you're in any webpage in Chrome,

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.330
you can pull up Google
Assistant and just ask it

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.760
for a summary of the entire
webpage you're reading.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:16.980
And Google Assistant
with this new AI features

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.620
reads your screen and generates a summary

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:21.120
of three or four bullet points

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.530
that are typically pretty good.

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:24.450
Now I wanna try this
with a few other apps,

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:26.310
but I basically always got an error saying

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:27.990
this app doesn't support summaries,

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:30.270
So it looks like the app developers

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:32.490
are gonna have to opt in
to have their apps scraped

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.440
by the summaries which I kind of suspect

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:35.790
a lot of them are not gonna do that.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.340
Even Google's own news
app doesn't support it.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:40.890
So this basically is just a Chrome thing.

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:41.880
And then after the summary,

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:44.430
it gives you a few cards
to ask follow up questions

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:47.070
about the subject and just
keep Googling more stuff.

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:48.900
But then just this phone

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.210
gets a lot of the little stuff right too.

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:54.300
Just the little things that
make it feel like a flagship.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:56.190
The haptics are excellent.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:58.320
Like it's got some of the
highest quality phone calls

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:00.570
I've ever heard on a
phone, stuff like that.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:03.990
Overall, when you put fit
and finish and hardware

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:06.390
up alongside software experience,

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:08.100
and I think these new screens especially,

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:11.040
it feels like this is finally
the generation of Pixel

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:14.220
that is right alongside
the other flagships

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:15.150
of the smartphone world.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.310
And it gets supported like a flagship too.

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:19.290
Seven years of software updates,

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:21.330
seven years of feature drop updates,

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:23.340
seven years of security updates,

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:25.350
seven years of spare parts available.

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.293
And it'll do so for the
new starting price of $700.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.050
And I think it's worth it.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:33.060
I honestly, I think this
is worth the new price tag.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:35.010
I think everything they've
done from the 120 hertz

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:36.870
and the brighter screen across the board,

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:39.570
this is a phone that I would
have no problem recommending.

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:43.830
Now, is the 300 extra
dollars for the Pro worth it?

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:44.790
That one's gonna be tough.

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:47.640
And again, it's because
Google did such a good job

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:50.850
with feature parity, same
chip, same primary camera

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:52.830
between these, so it's gonna come down

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:55.140
to the couple of small
differences between them.

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:58.230
Do you want that telephoto
camera and the bigger screen,

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:02.190
bigger battery, bigger phone,
the temperature sensor?

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:03.300
I don't know.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:05.670
It's harder to recommend
the Pro for most people,

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:08.150
but the Pixel 8, this is a good phone.

00:16:08.150 --> 00:16:09.300
So that's basically it.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:10.440
Thanks for watching.

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:11.820
Catch you guys in the next one.

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:12.653
Peace.

00:16:12.653 --> 00:16:15.064
(light music)

